# Copy to .env. podman compose reads it from this directory automatically.

# Credential for the control API and the CLI. There is no default: the gateway
# refuses to start without one.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
KEERA_OPERATOR_KEY=

# Encrypts the API keys operators type into the control panel, which is how a
# hosted model gets its credential at the moment somebody adds it, rather than
# through a variable below and a restart. The panel's field is disabled without
# one, so compose asks for one too. Changing it later makes the keys already
# stored unreadable, and they have to be entered again.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
KEERA_SECRET_KEY=

# Reads /metrics and nothing else. Without one, the only
# credential that can scrape is the operator key above - which also sets every
# guardrail in the deployment, and would then live in a scrape config.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#KEERA_METRICS_TOKEN=

# How long usage events and audit entries are kept. Both default to for ever,
# because usage is what billing reports from and the audit log is what a
# compliance reader audits - how long to keep either is your decision, not the
# gateway's. They are also the only two tables that grow with traffic, so make
# the decision rather than inheriting it. See docs/sizing.md.
#KEERA_USAGE_RETENTION=8760h
#KEERA_AUDIT_RETENTION=17520h

# How long an agent conversation may go quiet before the next request on it is
# read as a new task on the panel's Sessions screen. Nothing is stored per
# session, so this re-cuts every session already recorded rather than only the
# ones from here on - read a busy week at two or three values before settling.
# See docs/sessions.md.
#KEERA_SESSION_GAP=30m

# Interface the gateway's one port is published on. It carries the control API
# and the panel as well as the inference API - there is no second port to
# firewall them apart - so leave this at 127.0.0.1 until you have a real
# operator key above and a TLS terminator in front.
KEERA_BIND=127.0.0.1

# Hugging Face id of the model keera-engine loads. It is served under the
# fixed name in compose.yaml's --served-model-name regardless of what this is,
# so changing it needs no change to models.yaml.
KEERA_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-1.5B-Instruct

# vLLM image. Use docker.io/vllm/vllm-openai-cpu:latest-arm64 on aarch64. Both
# variables above apply only when compose.yaml is used on its own; the two
# override files below bring their own backend, model and image.
KEERA_ENGINE_IMAGE=docker.io/vllm/vllm-openai-cpu:latest-x86_64

# /dev/shm for the inference container. vLLM needs far more than podman's 64 MB
# default; raise this for larger models or tensor parallelism. This one is the
# base file's; the GPU tier has KEERA_GPU_SHM_SIZE below, which is separate
# precisely because this is set here and would otherwise shadow its default.
KEERA_SHM_SIZE=2gb

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployment tier
#
# Which backend serves the models is chosen by the override file passed on the
# command line, not by a variable here - so neither of the blocks below needs
# editing to bring the intended tier up.
#
#   laptop or demo, llama.cpp on CPU:
#     podman compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.cpu.yaml up -d --build
#   GPU host, vLLM:
#     podman compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.gpu.yaml up -d --build
#
# compose.yaml on its own is vLLM's CPU build, which is slower than llama.cpp on
# the same hardware and is kept only so the base file has no override.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# compose.cpu.yaml. A GGUF repository and, after the colon, the quantisation to
# pull from it; llama.cpp defaults to Q4_K_M when the tag is left off.
#KEERA_LLAMA_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-1.5B-Instruct-GGUF:Q4_K_M
#KEERA_LLAMA_IMAGE=ghcr.io/ggml-org/llama.cpp:server

# compose.gpu.yaml. ~5.6 GB of weights, so it fits a 24 GB card (g6.xlarge on
# AWS) with room for roughly eight 32k sessions. Drop the -AWQ suffix and add
# `--quantization fp8` to the command for a better trade on an Ada card, or name
# Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-14B-Instruct-AWQ (~9.9 GB) for a stronger model on the same
# card at one or two concurrent sessions. The unquantised 14B needs ~30 GB and a
# 48 GB card - g6e.xlarge.
#KEERA_GPU_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct-AWQ
#KEERA_VLLM_IMAGE=docker.io/vllm/vllm-openai:latest
# 8gb is the floor for a single card. Raise it for tensor parallelism.
#KEERA_GPU_SHM_SIZE=8gb

# text or json.
KEERA_LOG_FORMAT=text
KEERA_LOG_LEVEL=info

# Single sign-on for the control panel. Without these the panel accepts only the
# operator key above, and audit entries cannot name a person. See docs/sso.md.
#KEERA_OIDC_ISSUER=https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant>/v2.0
#KEERA_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
#KEERA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
#KEERA_OIDC_REDIRECT_URL=https://keera.example.ch/control/auth/callback
# Setting the first of these hands the administrator role to the directory for
# the whole gateway: it is read on every sign-in, and the panel, the CLI and the
# API stop assigning roles because the next sign-in would undo them. Leave it
# unset to make administrators with `keera user role` instead - which is the
# only shape that works on a provider issuing no groups at all, Google Workspace
# being one. The operator role is never assignable either way: it comes from the
# second of these or from KEERA_OPERATORS below.
#KEERA_OIDC_ADMIN_GROUPS=ai-platform-admins
#KEERA_OIDC_OPERATOR_GROUPS=keera-operators
# Role for a person who matches no group and no address below. See docs/sso.md.
#KEERA_OIDC_DEFAULT_ROLE=member

# Several directories at once, which is the hosted shape: customers who are not
# all on the same one, and a button per name on the sign-in screen. Setting this
# replaces the three variables above; everything else stays shared, and any of
# it can be overridden per provider under KEERA_OIDC_<NAME>_*.
#
# A name is chosen once. It goes in the sign-in URL and in every identity that
# provider writes, so changing one orphans everybody who signed in through it.
#KEERA_OIDC_PROVIDERS=google,entra
#KEERA_OIDC_GOOGLE_ISSUER=https://accounts.google.com
#KEERA_OIDC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
#KEERA_OIDC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
#KEERA_OIDC_ENTRA_ISSUER=https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant>/v2.0
#KEERA_OIDC_ENTRA_CLIENT_ID=
#KEERA_OIDC_ENTRA_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Entra's groups claim carries object GUIDs rather than names, and it vanishes
# altogether for anybody in too many groups - Keera refuses such a sign-in
# rather than quietly demoting them. App roles avoid both. See docs/sso.md.
#KEERA_OIDC_ENTRA_GROUPS_CLAIM=roles
#KEERA_OIDC_ENTRA_ADMIN_GROUPS=keera-admins
# The gateway as a browser reaches it. One listener serves the panel at /, the
# inference API under /api and the control API under /control, so this one
# address is all three: it is where a refused request sends the developer it
# stopped, and - with /api on the end - what the panel's "Connect a client"
# page puts into the configuration it hands out. Empty leaves both to the Host
# header, which is right for anything not behind a proxy that rewrites it.
#KEERA_PUBLIC_URL=https://keera.example.ch

# Emails that are operators. On a directory with no Keera groups in it - every
# Google Workspace one - this is the only way to be one, because the operator
# role is not assignable through the panel, the CLI or the API.
#KEERA_OPERATORS=you@example.ch

# The other way to give a hosted model its credential, for a key that comes from
# a Secret or a vault rather than from a person: models.yaml names this
# variable, which `provider: anthropic` does for you. Prompts sent to such an
# model leave your infrastructure; see docs/providers.md.
#ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-…

# The same for Infomaniak, which serves open-weight models from its own Swiss
# data centres. Its product id is part of the address rather than a secret, so
# it goes in models.yaml as product_id and not here.
#INFOMANIAK_API_KEY=…

# The same for stepping stone, which also serves open-weight models in
# Switzerland. It issues one key per model, so a second model needs its own
# variable, named in models.yaml as api_key_env.
#STEPPING_STONE_API_KEY=sk-…

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sandboxes
#
# The machines the gateway lends out. Off unless a driver is named.
#
# These are read by `make dev`, which runs the gateway as a process on this
# host - and that is the only shape the podman driver works in. The
# containerised gateway in compose.yaml cannot use it: it is a FROM scratch
# image with no shell, so it has no podman to run, and sandboxes created from
# inside it would be siblings of the gateway with whatever the host's podman can
# do, which is the opposite of what a sandbox is for.
#
# Before the first sandbox:  make sandbox-image
# See docs/sandboxes.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# podman, or empty for a deployment that lends out no sandboxes.
#KEERA_SANDBOX_DRIVER=podman

# The catalogue. `make dev` defaults this to the file below, so it only has to
# be set to point somewhere else.
#KEERA_SANDBOXES_FILE=compose/sandboxes.yaml

# The gateway as a sandbox reaches it, which is not the address you use.
# 127.0.0.1 inside a container is the container, so a sandbox pointed at it
# would find nothing listening and its agent would fail with a connection error
# rather than anything that names the cause. podman resolves
# host.containers.internal to this host from inside a container, and `make dev`
# defaults to it.
#
# It also means the gateway has to listen on more than loopback, which is what
# KEERA_ADDR=:8080 - the default - already does.
#KEERA_SANDBOX_PUBLIC_URL=http://host.containers.internal:8080

# What an agent inside a sandbox is pointed at. Empty leaves it to the client's
# own default, which for most is nothing usable.
#KEERA_SANDBOX_MODEL=keera-speed

# An OCI runtime for each isolation tier this host can deliver. A class asking
# for a tier with no runtime here is refused at creation rather than quietly run
# on the host kernel - a sandbox that claimed a kernel of its own and did not
# have one would be worse than the refusal. Most hosts have neither, and the
# development catalogue declares `isolation: standard` accordingly.
#KEERA_SANDBOX_RUNTIME_ISOLATED=runsc
#KEERA_SANDBOX_RUNTIME_VM=krun

# How long a sandbox nobody is attached to runs before it is suspended - the
# volume kept, the compute released. Empty leaves it running until it expires.
# Refused below 5m, because an editor attached over ssh sends nothing at all
# while its user is reading a diff.
#KEERA_SANDBOX_IDLE_SUSPEND=45m

# Where sandboxes check a repository out from: github or gitlab. Empty means a
# sandbox cannot be created with a repository in it. Secrets are read from
# files. See "Code in, work out" in docs/sandboxes.md.
#KEERA_SANDBOX_GIT_FORGE=gitlab
#KEERA_SANDBOX_GIT_URL=https://gitlab.example.ch
#KEERA_SANDBOX_GIT_TOKEN_FILE=/run/secrets/keera-gitlab-token
# For GitHub, a GitHub App instead of a token:
#KEERA_SANDBOX_GIT_APP_ID=123456
#KEERA_SANDBOX_GIT_APP_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/keera-github-app.pem
